{{Japanese name|Hirota}}

'''[[Kenshin Hirota]]''' (廣田 謙信, ''Hirota Kenshin''; 14 May 1938 – 20 September 2018) was a Sanese politician who served as the 49th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 2006 to 2009. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), his premiership was defined by his historic efforts to address the war crimes of the [[Great Adonian War]] and reconcile the nation's difficult history with its neighbors.

The son of former Prime Minister [[Kōki Hirota]]—who was executed as a Class A war criminal in 1953—[[Kenshin Hirota]]'s ascent to the highest office was initially met with shock and outrage across Eastern Fosia. However, he defied expectations by becoming the most apologetic leader in Sanese history, establishing the Truth and Reconciliation Commission and issuing the landmark "Okami Declaration" which formally accepted state responsibility for the war.

==Early life and education==
[[Kenshin Hirota]] was born in 1938 in the capital city of [[Okami]]. He was the youngest son of diplomat and politician [[Kōki Hirota]]. His early childhood was marked by the privations of the [[Great Adonian War]] and the subsequent collapse of the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]] government.

The defining moment of his life occurred when he was ten years old. His father, having served as Prime Minister during the early phases of the war, was arrested by the Allied Occupation forces, tried by the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, and executed by hanging in 1953. The stigma of being the "son of a war criminal" followed Kenshin throughout his youth. He was often bullied in school and excluded from social circles, an experience that he later cited as the foundation for his lifelong commitment to human rights and restorative justice.

He attended [[Okami University]], where he kept a low profile, studying constitutional law. He graduated in 1961 and briefly worked in a legal aid clinic before entering the private sector, deliberately avoiding the public eye for decades.

==Political career==
Hirota entered politics relatively late in life, running for the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in the 1979 general election. He chose to run in the [[Okami 1st District]], the heart of the capital, campaigning on a platform of judicial reform and civil liberties. His decision to run using his family name was controversial, but he won the seat by a narrow margin, appealing to voters who felt the post-war trials had been "victor's justice" while simultaneously promising never to repeat the mistakes of the past.

For the first twenty years of his legislative career, Hirota remained a backbencher. He gained a reputation as the "Silent Conscience" of the LDP, often voting against his own party on issues regarding state secrecy and police powers. He served as [[Minister of Justice (Okaiken)|Minister of Justice]] under Prime Minister [[Katsuya Okada]] from 2001 to 2003, where he oversaw significant reforms to the penal code and worked to improve the treatment of prisoners.

==Prime Minister (2006–2009)==
===The "Hirota Shock"===
In March 2006, Prime Minister [[Yoshito Sengoku]] resigned amid a corruption scandal. The LDP, fractured and seeking a leader who could project moral authority, turned to Hirota as a compromise candidate. His election as LDP President and subsequently Prime Minister was dubbed the "Hirota Shock" by the international press. Governments in [[Peilan]] and [[Kwangju]] issued strong diplomatic protests, fearing that the son of a wartime leader would seek to whitewash history.

===Truth and Reconciliation===
Contrary to the fears of neighboring nations, Hirota made historical reconciliation the centerpiece of his administration. In 2007, he pushed the Truth and Reconciliation Act through the Diet, establishing an independent commission to investigate state crimes committed between 1937 and 1952. Unlike previous inquiries, this commission had the power to subpoena government records and grant amnesty to whistleblowers.

In August 2008, on the anniversary of the war's end, Hirota delivered the Okami Declaration. In a televised address from the steps of the National Diet, he offered an unequivocal apology for the aggression and atrocities committed by the Empire of Mitoyagi. He specifically cited his father's failure to stop the military as a "personal and national shame," stating that "I bear the name of the past, but I serve the future."

===Domestic Policy and Resignation===
Domestically, Hirota pursued a moderate "social liberal" agenda. He increased funding for elderly care and attempted to address the gender gap in the workforce. However, his focus on historical issues alienated the conservative wing of the LDP, particularly the faction led by future Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]].

The global financial crisis of 2008 severely impacted Okaiken's export-driven economy. While Hirota's government passed a stimulus package, his approval ratings began to slide as unemployment rose. By late 2009, facing a rebellion from the nationalist right-wing of his party and declining health, Hirota announced his resignation. He was succeeded by his Deputy Prime Minister and close ally, [[Heitarō Seda]].

==Legacy==
[[Kenshin Hirota]] is regarded by historians as a transformative but tragic figure. His "Okami Declaration" remains the official stance of the Sanese government regarding the war, although it has been challenged by subsequent nationalist administrations. To the international community, he is remembered as the "Penitent Prime Minister" who successfully turned his family's dark legacy into a force for peace. To domestic conservatives, however, he is often viewed with suspicion for "apologizing too much."

He retired from politics in 2012 and died of natural causes in 2018.

